    "stem": "Aarav starts at $7$ and applies the Collatz rule: if the current term is even, halve it; if it is odd, take $3n+1$. He writes $7$, $22$, $11$, $34$, $17$, $52$, $26$, $13$, $40$, $20$, $10$, $5$, $16$, $8$, $4$, $2$, $1$.",
    "item_type": "structured",
    "options": {},
    "statements": [],
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": "i",
        "text": "Which claim correctly separates this chain from the Collatz conjecture?",
        "marks": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "The chain is one Collatz example that reaches $1$; it does not prove the rule for every whole-number start."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "Reaching $1$ from $7$ proves that every whole-number start reaches $1$."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "The Collatz rule applies only to even starts, so the odd terms such as $7$ and $11$ are mistakes."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ii",
        "text": "Give one reason for your choice.",
        "marks": 1
      }
    ],
